---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CTASection from '../components/CTASection.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../components/Disclaimer.astro';
import { BUSINESS, MEDICARE_2026 } from '../data/site';

const title = 'High-Deductible Plan G vs. Standard Plan G';
const description = 'High-Deductible Plan G and standard Plan G offer the same coverage — the difference is how you pay. Here\'s how to decide which setting fits you.';
const today = '2026-06-03';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    { '@type': 'Article', headline: title, description, author: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#agent` }, publisher: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#organization` }, datePublished: today, dateModified: today, mainEntityOfPage: `${BUSINESS.url}/high-deductible-plan-g-vs-plan-g` },
    { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/high-deductible-plan-g-vs-plan-g#webpage`, name: title, speakable: { '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification', cssSelector: ['.lead', 'h2'] } },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <PageHero
    title="High-Deductible Plan G vs. Standard Plan G"
    subtitle="Same coverage, different dial setting. Here's how to know which one fits you."
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Medicare Plans', href: '/services' }, { label: 'High-Deductible Medigap', href: '/services/high-deductible-medicare-supplement' }, { label: 'HD Plan G vs. Plan G' }]}
  />

  <article class="py-14 lg:py-18">
    <div class="container-narrow prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:text-crst-primary prose-a:text-crst-accent">

      <div class="not-prose mb-8 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-crst-gold bg-navy-50 p-5">
        <p class="text-navy-800 leading-relaxed m-0">
          <strong>High-Deductible Plan G and standard Plan G offer the same standardized benefits.</strong> The difference is
          how you pay: High-Deductible Plan G has a lower premium and a <strong>{MEDICARE_2026.hdDeductible}</strong> annual
          deductible ({MEDICARE_2026.year}) before full coverage begins. Standard Plan G has a higher monthly premium with a
          lower deductible ({MEDICARE_2026.partBDeductible} in {MEDICARE_2026.year}).
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="lead text-xl text-navy-700">
        These two plans aren't really different coverage — they're the same coverage with the dial set differently between
        premium and deductible. Knowing which setting fits you is the whole decision.
      </p>

      <h2>What's the same (almost everything)</h2>
      <p>
        Medigap plans are standardized by law, so a Plan G is a Plan G. Both versions cover the same things, both let you
        use any doctor or hospital in the country that accepts Medicare, and both pay 100% of Medicare-approved costs once
        you've met your deductible for the year. There are no networks or referrals with either one.
      </p>

      <h2>What's different (premium vs. deductible)</h2>

      <div class="not-prose grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-crst-primary p-5 text-white">
          <h3 class="font-heading font-semibold text-crst-gold mb-3 mt-0">High-Deductible Plan G</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-white/85 list-none p-0 m-0">
            <li>• Lower monthly premium</li>
            <li>• You pay covered costs up to {MEDICARE_2026.hdDeductible} ({MEDICARE_2026.year}), then the plan pays 100%</li>
            <li>• Potential for lower total annual cost</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-navy-100 bg-navy-50 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-heading font-semibold text-crst-primary mb-3 mt-0">Standard Plan G</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-navy-600 list-none p-0 m-0">
            <li>• Higher monthly premium</li>
            <li>• Low out-of-pocket costs once the Part B deductible ({MEDICARE_2026.partBDeductible}) is met</li>
            <li>• Most hands-off; predictable spending</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>The cost trade-off in plain terms</h2>
      <p>
        Neither is "better" in the abstract — it depends on you. With High-Deductible Plan G you pay a lower premium, then
        cover costs up to {MEDICARE_2026.hdDeductible} before the plan pays 100%. With standard Plan G you pay a higher
        premium every month and less when you use care. The right choice is whichever gives you the lower total annual cost
        for the way you actually use care.
      </p>

      <h2>How the deductible works over a year</h2>
      <p>
        Think of it as a running tally. Early in the year, you pay your share of Medicare-covered costs yourself, and each
        payment moves you closer to the deductible. The moment your covered costs reach {MEDICARE_2026.hdDeductible}, the
        plan takes over and pays 100% of Medicare-approved charges for the rest of the year — just like standard Plan G.
      </p>

      <h2>Does the Part B deductible count toward it?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. The {MEDICARE_2026.year} Part B deductible of {MEDICARE_2026.partBDeductible} counts toward your {MEDICARE_2026.hdDeductible} high-deductible amount.
      </p>

      <h2>When does the deductible reset?</h2>
      <p>
        It resets at the start of each calendar year. Whatever you spent toward the deductible last year does not carry
        forward. Medicare also adjusts the deductible amount annually, so the figure changes from one year to the next.
      </p>

      <h2>Switching between them later</h2>
      <p>
        Where you live decides how easily you can change your mind. In New York and Connecticut, you can move between
        standard and high-deductible Plan G any time of year with no health questions. In most other states, switching after
        your initial 6-month Medigap window usually requires medical underwriting, and you could be declined — so the choice
        carries more weight there.
      </p>

      <h2>A note on eligibility (Plan F vs. Plan G)</h2>
      <p>
        If you became eligible for Medicare before January 1, 2020, you may also see a high-deductible Plan F. Everyone who
        became eligible on or after that date uses Plan G instead.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 rounded-xl bg-crst-primary p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-white font-heading text-lg mb-2 m-0">Standard or high-deductible? Let's look at your numbers.</p>
        <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mb-4 m-0">A no-cost, no-pressure comparison.</p>
        <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="btn-accent text-base px-7 py-3">Call {BUSINESS.phone}</a>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <Disclaimer variant="tpmo" />
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          {MEDICARE_2026.year} figures are set by Medicare and change annually. Plan availability and pricing vary by insurer
          and location. NY producer license #: {BUSINESS.nyLicense}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection headline="Let's find the right setting for your situation." />
</BaseLayout>
